# mm.touch

A portable touch input interface. It reports where a panel is being touched and
nothing more: gesture recognition, event queues, and pointer state are policy
above this module, and controller registers are detail below it.

read fills as many points as the panel currently reports, up to the caller's
span, and sets count. No contact is Ok with count zero rather than an error:
nothing being touched is an answer. A caller never learns about points it had no
room for, so a span of one is a valid single-touch client of a five-point panel.

Coordinates are in the panel's own space, which geometry describes. Rotation and
calibration belong to the caller, because only the caller knows how the panel is
mounted relative to what is drawn on it.

The interrupt line a controller may offer is deliberately absent. A caller polls
read; a provider may use its own interrupt internally to make that cheap.
Exposing an interrupt would mean exposing a callback registration mm.mcu does
not have.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board touch provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.touch.
